# Portal / PDF literals — do not “clean up”

German (and LOGA3) strings in these files are **match targets** against a foreign UI or PDF, not app chrome.

## Layers

| Literals | Layer | Change when |
|----------|--------|-------------|
| `öffnen`, `Zeiten`, Zeitprotokoll, GWT DOM, layout CSS | **Source plugin** `src/sources/loga3/` | LOGA3 portal UI changes (live smoke required) |
| `Abrechnungsmonat`, `Übertrag…`, duty codes in parser/pdfText | **Parser** via pack `parserId` | That employer’s PDF layout changes |
| Mapping JSON under `src/packs/builtin/` | **Pack** | Codes/colors/presets for that AG |

Pack ≠ portal automation. Same LOGA3 site can serve many AGs; a new AG usually needs a **new parser/pack**, not new i18n keys for selectors.

## Rules

1. **Never** move portal/PDF literals into `src/i18n/` or wrap them in `t()`.
2. **Never** rename/“English-ify” selector text, aria-labels, or PDF regexes for style — Fetch/parse will break.
3. Touch only with an intentional portal/PDF change + live or fixture proof (matrix/smoke/unit fixture).
4. App-facing copy (buttons, errors, widgets) stays in i18n; that is a different layer.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — looks like Denglish cleanup
el => t('open') === textOf(el)

// ✅ GOOD — match the live LOGA3 label
el => /^öffnen$/i.test(textOf(el))
```
